A||||14||fieroburton||buying a new altirnater||||1||
Z||000000||fieroburton||07-29-2001||09:41 PM||||recently  as some of you know I am having problems with my fiero charging system . I am now having to replace my alternator . a guy told me to get one from autozone for 60 dollars with a life time guarantee . this sounds good so I'm now saving money for that and soon I'm going to replace my old one . I'm only 3 weeks away from being 16 and  have little to non experience about cars and don't want to screw up my only car I have  before I can drive it. so I know this forum is a great source to get help and info for doing things to fiero's.<BR>so if any body has any information to share I would greatly appreciate it ! also what can I do to make positive shore that my alternator needs to be changed ?<BR>sorry for any spelling errors<BR>and thank you<P>------------------<BR>1984 2M4 SE <BR>    <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroFanaticus2||07-29-2001||10:04 PM||||Just be careful when removing old rusted bolts, too much torque and you may bust the heads off of them.  I learned from experience.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dump_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroburton||07-29-2001||10:05 PM||||how did you take out the alternator out ?<P>------------------<BR>1984 2M4 SE <BR>    <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSoul||07-29-2001||10:14 PM||||  You have to first remove the dog bone to get clearance. Then it is just a couple of bolts and some wires.  If you don't already have a Haynes manual you should get one, they are lots of help (with pictures).  You can get one for about $12 at Autozone or Pep Boys.  Good Luck.||||reg||6
Z||000004||V8Fiero||07-29-2001||10:23 PM||||Do you have a 6cyl or 4cyl?<P>Ive had my altenator out three times in the last 2 weeks. Had a bad one, so I put a new one in. Lasted one week and started to fail, so I pulled it and took it back and they tested it and said it was fine. So.....I put it back in. Still acted funny, so I bought a new battery (old one was only 4months old, free replacement). Still wasnt it. So.....I took the new alternator back and demanded an new one to replace the older "new" one with.<P>Ive gotten it down to under 1 hour taking out and putting it back in..........<P>I have the six cylinder. You have to remove from under the engine.<P>Jack the car up on the rear passenger side. Take off the wheel. Disconnect the tie rod from the spindle.<BR>From the top of the engine compartment there are 3 15mm bolts that hold on the alternator bracket. Take these out. Also there is one 18mm bolt also that holds in the bracket and also is a motor mount bolt. This too must be removed. All that is left it the two bolts that hold the alt to the alt bracket. One 15mm and a 13mm on the bottom. (this is the belt tightening bolt) Remove both pieces from the passenger rear fender well.<P>That is how I did it. I did try to remove from the top. NO WAY was that happening.<P>Hope this helps any.<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Gold<BR>86 GT Red <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RedRavensig.JPG"> <BR>"Trying to change from V8Fiero to RedRavenGT"||||reg||5
Z||000005||fiero56||07-29-2001||10:42 PM||||Do you have an auto tranny, Scott?<P>I was in the same situation as yourself very recently, and I never had to deal with jacking the car up, or separating tie-rods, etc. This was with my '86 2.8 4-speed. With Fiero on ground, reach behind wheel and remove adjuster nut, reach from above engine, unplug wiring, and remove single bolt that attaches alt to mount. The alt falls on the ground, installation is reverse, takes about 7 minutes.<P>He has a Duke according to his sig, so I have no idea about the 4-cyl, but I wouldn't think it would be much different.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroburton||07-29-2001||10:50 PM||||yes I have a 84 duke . does 60 dollars sound good for a alternator ? it has life time guarantee .<BR><P>------------------<BR>1984 2M4 SE <BR>    <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||07-29-2001||11:08 PM||||yes $60.00 is a good price for a alternater with a life time warranty from auto zone.just out of curiosity how much experience do you have working on cars ?and what kind of tools do you have access to.please let us know so we can better help you.but this task you are about to perform is some what easy if you have alittle patience .yes there are people that probaly can perform this replacement in ten minutes or so.but I have some pretty good experince working on cars ,and it would take me at least 1 hour to do this job not because it is hard but because I like to take my time and enjoy what I am doing to my car.and theres less chance of screwing something up in the process.and when you are having a hard time while you are doing something on your car just walk for a little while and regroup,rather then fighting the problem.and remember we are here to help you any way that we can.<p>[This message has been edited by starfighter007@msn.com (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000008||V8Fiero||07-30-2001||12:12 AM||||fiero56:<P>Yep, auto tranny.<P><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Gold<BR>86 GT Red <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RedRavensig.JPG"> <BR>"Trying to change from V8Fiero to RedRavenGT"||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||07-30-2001||12:37 AM||||Most of the time, it is best to remove the negative battery cable before doing any work on the electrical system. Press down on the alternator belt and make a note of how tight it is. Pushing down, the belt should deflect beetween 1/4" to 1/2" depending how far apart the pulleys are(From Hanynes manual) Take the dogbone strut off if you think it is in the way.(it will make access easier) There is a set of wires that slip on in the back of the alternator and a larger wire that is held on with a nut. Both have to be removed. Pay attention how the slip on connection comes off. It usually will only go on one way, unless it has been altered in some way. Remove the bolt that allows adjustment of the belt. Remove the other bolt and nut that<BR>hold the alternator in place. You may have to loosen the alternator bracket also, I can't remember. I took the alternator out the bottom to get it out. Make sure you don't disturb the wires on the tach unit or the oil presure sender. Take the alternator and have it tested at AutoZone or any good parts shop. If it is bad, installation is reverse of disassembly.Don't over-tighten the wire that bolts to the back of the alt, be sure to press the rubber boot back over the nut and wire connector. It is easier to put the wires on while the alt is resting on the bracket, but not bolted up. Get a manual as soon as you can, it will help a lot. These notes are simplified, I don't mean to infer you don't know all this already. Good luck<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroburton||07-30-2001||01:05 AM||||yes I have a hayns Manuel and I'm reading over it now.<BR>the only tools I have are some socket wrenches and pliers , (a basic kit ).<BR>so is it a good idea to jack the car up?<BR>and how do you take off the air intake ?<BR>I really appreciate all of you helping me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1984 2M4 SE <BR>    <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||07-30-2001||02:45 AM||||two nuts hold the air cleaner on. disconnect the flex hose from the exh manifold to the bottom of the air cleaner housing(called the stove or heat duct), release the clamp on tthe left rear of the eng compartment(Front facing the eng) that brings in fresh air. Cover the inlet of the throttle body with a rag or something else so you dont drop anything down into it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Hank is Here||07-30-2001||10:03 AM||||The only thing I would say is that I believe that all 84´s had the one wire altenator.  Easy as heck to rebiuld yourself, if you know how.  The later alternators are a pain, and cannot easily be rebuilt at home.  If I remember correctly the last  one wire kit I got cost me around 8 bucks and took about 20 min to assemble and rebuild.  I have only done three ever, but they are easy.<P>Mark  ||||reg||1
